{htxt id="id-dashboard_intro"}
    {ts}
        <p>The Caseload Dashboard is designed to give you insight into your district's current caseload, help you make decisions about case assignment, and identify when case managers need extra support.</p>
    {/ts}
{/htxt}
{htxt id="id-overview-totals"}
    {ts}
        <p>A look at district wide case stats:
        <ul>
            <li>Total Open &dash; includes cases with statuses: Ongoing, Urgent, Not Started, Assigned, and Unassigned</li>
            <li>Total Urgent &dash; cases with the status "Urgent"</li>
            <li>Total Unassigned &dash; cases that either (a) have the status 'unassigned' or (b) are open and do not have an associated case manager</li>
            <li>Open < 7 Days &dash; Unresolved cases that have been open less than 7 days</li>
            <li>Open 7 - 30 Days &dash; Unresolved cases that have been open between 7 and 30 days</li>
            <li>Open > 30 Days &dash; Unresolved cases that have been open longer than 30 days</li>
            <li>Resolved Last 7 Days &dash; Cases that have been closed/resolved in the last 7 days</li>
            <li>Avg. Days to Resolution &dash; Your district's average case resolution rate. This average includes cases that are up to 180 days old.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
        <p>Each total in the caseload overview can be clicked to reveal more details about the cases included in the total.</p>
    {/ts}
{/htxt}
{htxt id="id-casemanager-totals"}
{ts}
    <p>A look at each case manager's cases.</p>
    <p>Each total can be clicked to show more details about the cases included in the total.</p>
    <p>Notes:
    <ul>
        <li>Open cases include cases with statuses: Ongoing, Urgent, Not Started, Assigned, and Unassigned. </li>
        <li>Cases assigned to multiple case managers, will be included in all of those case managers' stats.</li>
        <li>Average Days to Resolution includes cases that are up to 180 days old.</li>
        <li>Case Manager stats reflect case manager assignment &mdash; not case manager activity. For example, Mel resolves a case that is assigned to Kha. This resolution will be included in Kha's stats &mdash; not Mel's &mdash; because Kha is the current case manager.</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
{/ts}
{/htxt}